1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:01,610
<u>Caching & Data Freshness</u>

2
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:03,750
<i>What is a cache?</i>

3
00:00:05,002 --> 00:00:08,772
A cache stores a reusable copy of data closer to the consumer or in a faster medium.

4
00:00:09,472 --> 00:00:14,312
It reduces latency or source load, but creates freshness, invalidation, eviction, and failure questions.

5
00:00:15,825 --> 00:00:16,725
<i>What is a cache hit?</i>

6
00:00:18,238 --> 00:00:21,688
A cache hit happens when the requested value is found and can be served from the cache.

7
00:00:22,398 --> 00:00:24,718
A cache miss falls through to the underlying source of truth.

8
00:00:26,238 --> 00:00:27,228
<i>What is cache-aside?</i>

9
00:00:28,771 --> 00:00:32,811
In cache-aside, the application explicitly checks the cache and loads the source of truth on a miss.

10
00:00:33,521 --> 00:00:35,331
The client sends a request to the service.

11
00:00:36,031 --> 00:00:37,501
The service checks the cache first.

12
00:00:38,201 --> 00:00:40,691
If the value is present, it can return it immediately.

13
00:00:41,401 --> 00:00:44,871
On a cache miss, the service reads the data from the database, stores it in the cache,

14
00:00:45,071 --> 00:00:46,301
and then returns it to the client.

15
00:00:47,830 --> 00:00:48,740
<i>What is TTL?</i>

16
00:00:50,267 --> 00:00:53,477
TTL (Time To Live) limits how long a cached entry may be reused.

17
00:00:54,177 --> 00:00:57,627
Shorter TTLs usually improve freshness but increase cache misses and source load.

18
00:00:59,510 --> 00:01:00,770
<i>What is cache invalidation?</i>

19
00:01:02,018 --> 00:01:05,708
Cache invalidation removes or expires cached data when the authoritative value changes.

20
00:01:06,398 --> 00:01:10,348
The difficult part is handling races, failed invalidations, and multiple related keys.

21
00:01:11,857 --> 00:01:12,627
<i>What is eviction?</i>

22
00:01:14,155 --> 00:01:17,625
Eviction removes entries because they expire or the cache reaches a resource limit.

23
00:01:18,335 --> 00:01:21,755
Eviction policy affects hit rate, memory usage, and which data stays hot.

24
00:01:23,273 --> 00:01:24,243
<i>What is cache warming?</i>

25
00:01:25,763 --> 00:01:28,643
Cache warming preloads likely-needed entries before demand arrives.

26
00:01:29,343 --> 00:01:33,563
It can reduce cold-start misses, but broad warming can waste resources or overload dependencies.

27
00:01:35,097 --> 00:01:36,497
<i>What is stale-while-revalidate?</i>

28
00:01:38,038 --> 00:01:42,178
Stale-while-revalidate serves a bounded stale value immediately and refreshes it in the background.

29
00:01:42,878 --> 00:01:45,718
It trades some freshness for lower latency and better availability.

30
00:01:47,238 --> 00:01:48,368
<i>What is negative caching?</i>

31
00:01:49,891 --> 00:01:53,971
Negative caching stores a known negative result, such as “resource not found,” for a short period.

32
00:01:54,671 --> 00:01:59,511
Transient network, server, or authorization failures should not be cached blindly because that can hide recovery.

33
00:02:01,385 --> 00:02:02,575
<i>What is a cache stampede?</i>

34
00:02:03,826 --> 00:02:09,046
A cache stampede, or thundering herd, happens when many callers reload the same popular value at once after expiration.

35
00:02:09,746 --> 00:02:14,016
Request coalescing, jittered TTLs, bounded stale serving, or controlled warming can reduce it.

36
00:02:15,537 --> 00:02:17,037
<i>What can happen when a cache fails?</i>

37
00:02:18,555 --> 00:02:22,665
If a cache becomes unavailable, traffic may suddenly fall through to the database or another source.

38
00:02:23,375 --> 00:02:25,615
Capacity planning must include this degraded mode.

39
00:02:27,121 --> 00:02:28,471
<i>What is a client-side cache?</i>

40
00:02:29,985 --> 00:02:34,685
A client-side cache keeps data on the device to reduce latency and network usage and can support offline behavior.

41
00:02:35,395 --> 00:02:39,575
It is still a replicated view and needs explicit refresh, staleness, storage, and cleanup rules.

42
00:02:41,105 --> 00:02:42,935
<i>What does freshness mean for mobile UI?</i>

43
00:02:44,459 --> 00:02:46,919
Freshness is a product contract, not just a timestamp.

44
00:02:47,629 --> 00:02:50,369
The UI may need to show when data is stale, when local writes are pending,

45
00:02:50,559 --> 00:02:52,249
and when the latest server state is not yet known.
